Look over the saxpy.c code.

1.) The following saxpy.c code uses the same variant method shown in
    simp_variant.c.  Moreover it requires mapping data to the device.  
    In many cases, storage is often made persistent thoughout many GPU 
    offloads. Here, too, a target_data in main() makes x and y data 
    persistent throughout target_data region (block), by mapping array
    sections of the x and y pointers.

2.) Note, the use_device_ptr(x,y) on the map clause instructs that
    x and y device pointers should be used, in the target_data region,
    for target offloading. In the d_saxpy() target directive they
    are specified to be device pointers. (The is_device_ptr may
    also be specified on the dispatch directive.)

a.) Compile and run saxpy on a GPU or PVC device.

Compiling:  Use icx Stampede3 and gcc on Vista 
            (nvidia hasn't implemented dispatch/match !)
Stampede3: icx -qopenmp -fiopenmp -fopenmp-targets=spir64  saxpy.c
    Vista: ml gcc; gcc -fopenmp -O3 -foffload=nvptx-none 
